    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d806">

                <pb facs="1085" n="983" sameAs="frus:frus1933v01/pg983"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">511.4C1/58: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                            Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1933-10-05T12:00:00+09:00">October 5,
                            1933—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received October 5—12:33 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>151. Department’s instruction No. 358, September 11, 1933, Convention of Saint
                    Germain-en-Laye. Foreign Office states Japanese Government willing to inform
                    French Government that Japanese Government has no objection to the understanding
                    upon which the United States ratified the convention provided we ask in an
                    official note that this be done. Is there any objection to my doing so?<anchor
                        n="11" xml:id="d806-f1"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d806-f1" n="11" xml:id="d806-f1-content">
                <p>Telegram No. 91, October 9, to the Ambassador in Japan reads: “Please do so.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
